    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "When Russian tanks rolled into Ukraine on February 24th, 2022, the United States government acted swiftly to provide support critical for Ukraine’s survival. But it was not the only American organization that did so. Without direct instruction from the U.S. government, without the same degree of advance warning, and without any prior experience, large American tech companies provided Ukraine free services in cybersecurity, cloud computing, and satellite communications. Focusing on Microsoft, Google, Amazon Web Services (AWS), and SpaceX, this thesis analyzes the role that these companies played in supporting Ukraine to uncover the incentives that drove their decisions. It retraces the path of these complex decisions at three levels: the firms themselves, the individuals who work at these firms, and the processes by which those individuals coordinated their efforts with the U.S. government. It builds a history of their involvement and decisions from publicly available information – including public statements, news articles, and other publications – and from 25 interviews with individuals from the four firms, the tech industry, the U.S. government, and the Ukrainian government. Using existing literature on corporate decision-making, this thesis proposes three explanations for corporate involvement in Ukraine: “profit seeking,” “moral obligation,” and “national security.” Due to several factors, including the need for urgent decision-making and the power of individuals, this thesis finds that no one theory explains these firms’ decisions but that all three hold credence. At times, this thesis separates its analysis of SpaceX’s Starlink, whose precarious arrangement with the Ukrainians eventually resulted in a chaotic transition of control to the purview of the U.S. government. Providing an early analysis of the unique power that tech companies wield in geopolitics, this thesis offers guidance to policymakers that navigate public-private partnerships with tech firms to align their actions with national security interests.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
